 GIL build the workers take turns and the reports are the same.
 
 ## Speed
@@ -123,16 +136,23 @@ Compiled, book of 1,050 policies (the seven new-business points 150 times), ESG
 years 0 to 2, so 1,050 × 3 × 13 = 40,950 valuations; median of three rounds, each run in a
 fresh process, on a 4-core cloud VM, Python 3.14.6 free-threaded, gcc 13:
 
-| Workers | Seconds | Speed-up |
-| ---: | ---: | ---: |
-| 1 | 8.71 | 1.00 |
-| 4 | 3.26 | 2.67 |
-
-The three rounds ranged 8.37–8.95 s and 3.02–3.36 s. Totals: DV01 129,960.616141 and
+| Route | Workers | Seconds | CPU seconds | Peak memory | Speed-up |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| pooled driver | 1 | 9.97 | 9.9 | 0.25 GB | 1.00 |
+| pooled driver | 4 | 3.80 | 13.4 | 0.35 GB | 2.62 |
+| collect | 1 | 9.81 | 9.7 | 1.1 GB | 1.00 |
+| collect | 4 | 3.75 | 12.5 | 1.1 GB | 2.62 |
+
+Median of five rounds, routes interleaved, with `Data` and `Actual` locked. Collect takes the
+same time as the pooled driver and about 1.1 GB against 0.25 GB, because the 1,050 `Actual`
+ItemSpaces stay alive across the 39 collects, where the pooled driver deletes each policy's
+once that policy is done. What collect gains is less model code: the report reads each close's
+total from one call instead of a per-policy task, a thread pool and its own sums. Totals: DV01 129,960.616141 and
 CS01 157,856.970872 in every run. Live modelx runs the 14-point book in about 300 s.
 
